      "narrative": "Pinal County’s residential market is currently slightly softening on prices but remains active, with the median sale price around $383,849 in May 2026, down 0.3% year over year.[1] Homes are taking longer to sell, averaging 71 days on market versus 67 days a year earlier, yet monthly sales volume has increased (1,006 homes sold in May versus 947 last year), indicating continued buyer demand.[1] Sale-to-list ratios around 98.4% suggest a more balanced buyer-seller dynamic, with modest discounting rather than aggressive bidding wars.[1] Local demand is supported by ongoing growth in communities such as San Tan Valley, Maricopa, and Queen Creek, which attract commuters to the Phoenix metro employers and benefit from new residential construction, though specific employer and migration figures are not detailed in the cited sources.[1][4][6]",
