      "narrative": "Pima County’s residential market is currently showing flat to slightly declining prices, with Tucson-area median sale prices essentially unchanged or modestly down year over year in mid-2026.[3][5][9][12] Inventory has risen compared with 2023 but remains around 3-4 months of supply, which local analyses describe as enough to give buyers more negotiating leverage without fully shifting pricing power away from sellers.[3][5] Market reports for early August 2026 characterize Pima County as having a “slight seller’s advantage,” with a high share of listings cutting prices and homes spending about 70 days on market, indicating a more balanced, slower-paced environment than the recent boom.[1][5] Local drivers include steady demand tied to the Tucson metro’s large public-sector and defense employers, ongoing in-migration to the region, and continued new construction that is adding to active inventory without triggering broad price declines.[3][5][9]",
